libpam-faciolock for Debian and Ubuntu
======================================

This package installs the PAM module and registers a pam-auth-update profile at
"Default: yes". A fresh install enables face authentication; until a face is
enrolled, the PAM stack falls through to the password prompt. An administrator's
explicitly disabled profile stays disabled on upgrades.

Do not hand-edit /etc/pam.d/*
-----------------------------

The profile is the supported route, and it is reversible by the same tool that
applied it:

  sudo faciolockctl disable
  sudo faciolockctl enable

One profile covers sudo, su, login, sddm, the KDE lock screen and polkit,
because all six include common-auth.

For the KDE lock screen, add your account to faciolock-auth and log out and
back in once so the session receives the new group membership:

  sudo gpasswd -a $USER faciolock-auth

Recovery
--------

Before making any PAM change, open a second terminal and hold a root shell in
it:

  sudo -i

That shell authenticated before the edit and does not care what /etc/pam.d says
afterwards. From it, disable the profile:

  faciolockctl disable

If every terminal is lost as well, the recovery is a root shell from the boot
menu: hold Shift for GRUB, choose the recovery entry, and /etc/pam.d is an
ordinary directory on an ordinary filesystem. Nothing in this design can make a
machine unbootable. The worst it can do is make one service refuse to
authenticate, and every refusal path falls through to the password prompt by
construction: "default=ignore" means daemon down, socket missing, not enrolled,
rate-limited, refused, malformed reply and a panic caught at the FFI boundary
all land on the next line of the stack, which is your password.

 -- Somesh Rao <cokasomeshrao@outlook.com>
